{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:7479976 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:7479976 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"7479976","source":"MED","pmid":"7479976","pmcid":"PMC40611","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC40611"]},"doi":"10.1073/pnas.92.24.11259","title":"Signal-induced degradation of I kappa B alpha requires site-specific ubiquitination.","authorString":"Scherer DC, Brockman JA, Chen Z, Maniatis T, Ballard DW.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Scherer DC","lastName":"Scherer","firstName":"D C","initials":"DC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Howard Hughes Medical Institute, Vanderbilt University School of Medicine, Nashville, TN 37232, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Brockman JA","lastName":"Brockman","firstName":"J A","initials":"JA"},{"fullName":"Chen Z","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Z","initials":"Z"},{"fullName":"Maniatis T","lastName":"Maniatis","firstName":"T","initials":"T"},{"fullName":"Ballard DW","lastName":"Ballard","firstName":"D W","initials":"DW"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"24","volume":"92","journalIssueId":755419,"dateOfPublication":"1995 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":1995,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","essn":"1091-6490","issn":"0027-8424","nlmid":"7505876","isoabbreviation":"Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A"},"printPublicationDate":"1995-11-01"},"pubYear":"1995","pageInfo":"11259-11263","abstractText":"The inhibitor protein I kappa B alpha controls the nuclear import of the transcription factor NF-kappa B. The inhibitory activity of I kappa B alpha is regulated from the cytoplasmic compartment by signal-induced proteolysis. Previous studies have shown that signal-dependent phosphorylation of serine residues 32 and 36 targets I kappa B alpha to the ubiquitin-proteasome pathway. Here we provide evidence that lysine residues 21 and 22 serve as the primary sites for signal-induced ubiquitination of I kappa B alpha. Conservative Lys-->Arg substitutions at both Lys-21 and Lys-22 produce dominant-negative mutants of I kappa B alpha in vivo. These constitutive inhibitors are appropriately phosphorylated but fail to release NF-kappa B in response to multiple inducers, including viral proteins, cytokines, and agents that mimic antigenic stimulation through the T-cell receptor. Moreover, these Lys-->Arg mutations prevent signal-dependent degradation of I kappa B alpha in vivo and ubiquitin conjugation in vitro. We conclude that site-specific ubiquitination of phosphorylated I kappa B alpha at Lys-21 and/or Lys-22 is an obligatory step in the activation of NF-kappa B.","affiliation":"Department of Microbiology and Immunology, Howard Hughes Medical Institute, Vanderbilt University School of Medicine, Nashville, TN 37232, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, U.S. Gov't, P.H.S.","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"T32 CA09385","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cells, Cultured"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Nucleus","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Multienzyme Complexes","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Proteasome Endopeptidase Complex"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cysteine Endopeptidases","meshQual